 "path": "memory/continual-arc-overlap-masks.md",
 "content": "# Continual-ARC: intersecting two binary masks\n\nObserved reusable rule (task-6e926b58, solved): A grid may encode two same-sized binary masks using distinct nonzero colors, separated either horizontally by a one-column divider or vertically by a divider row. Align the two halves cell-by-cell; output a grid of the shared dimensions with color 2 exactly where BOTH corresponding cells are nonzero, and 0 otherwise. Ignore the divider and original colors. Validate the orientation and dimensions from the demo: a 13x21 demo had two 13x10 masks separated by color 7; its 13x10 output was their pixelwise intersection. In a 27x3 test, two 13x3 masks were stacked around a row of 3s; output was the pixelwise intersection of the top color-4 mask and bottom color-5 mask. Example: output[r][c] = 2 iff upper/left[r][c] != 0 and lower/right[r][c] != 0, else 0.\n"
}
